\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Model. (a) A spin body of $n=4$ spins. Intra-body AF exchange $Jx+Kx^2$ ($x=\mathbf  {S}_i\cdot \mathbf  {S}_j$) favors the regular tetrahedron. (b) Bodies on a lattice are coupled by $-\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _{i,j}[G x^3+H_4 x^4]$ between \emph  {all} pairs of spins of neighboring bodies, so the coupling cannot tell which spin is which. (c) Channel spectrum for $J=1$, $K=0.5$, $zG=1$, $zH_4=0.5$: intra coefficients $f_l$, inter coefficients $-zg_l$, and their sum $\kappa _l$. Odd channels ($l=1,3$) carry magnetization; even channels ($l=2,4$) carry nematic order.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:model}{{1}{3}{Model. (a) A spin body of $n=4$ spins. Intra-body AF exchange $Jx+Kx^2$ ($x=\Sv _i\cdot \Sv _j$) favors the regular tetrahedron. (b) Bodies on a lattice are coupled by $-\sum _{i,j}[G x^3+H_4 x^4]$ between \emph {all} pairs of spins of neighboring bodies, so the coupling cannot tell which spin is which. (c) Channel spectrum for $J=1$, $K=0.5$, $zG=1$, $zH_4=0.5$: intra coefficients $f_l$, inter coefficients $-zg_l$, and their sum $\kappa _l$. Odd channels ($l=1,3$) carry magnetization; even channels ($l=2,4$) carry nematic order}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:DF}{{8}{3}{}{equation.3.8}{}}
